              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Henry VIII</strong><br>\nHenry VIII was born on June 28, 1491, in Greenwich, England. He was the second son of Henry VII and Elizabeth of York. Henry was not initially expected to become king, as he had an older brother, Arthur. However, Arthur's death in 1502 changed Henry's destiny. He received an excellent education and excelled in languages and the arts. Henry became king in 1509 after his father's death and was known for his charismatic personality and strong athletic physique.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Henry's Marriages and Quest for a Male Heir</strong><br>\nHenry VIII is famous for his six marriages, which he entered into with the hope of securing a male heir to continue the Tudor dynasty. His first marriage to Catherine of Aragon lasted 24 years but did not produce a surviving male heir, only a daughter, Mary. Henry's desperation for a son led him to seek an annulment from Catherine, setting off a chain of events that would reshape England's religious landscape.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Break with Rome</strong><br>\nWhen the Pope refused to annul his marriage to Catherine, Henry challenged the Church's authority. He declared himself the Supreme Head of the Church of England in 1534. This act separated the English Church from the Roman Catholic Church and led to the dissolution of monasteries. The break with Rome allowed Henry to marry Anne Boleyn, with whom he had hoped to have a male heir. Anne gave birth to Elizabeth, who later became one of England's greatest queens.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
