            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Execution of Anne Boleyn</strong><br>\nHenry's marriage to Anne Boleyn deteriorated when she also failed to provide a male heir. Anne was accused of adultery, incest, and high treason. Despite the lack of conclusive evidence, she was executed in 1536. Henry's relentless pursuit of a male heir and his willingness to change his queens and the country's religion marked him as a ruthless and powerful monarch.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Marriage to Jane Seymour</strong><br>\nShortly after Anne Boleyn's execution, Henry married Jane Seymour. Jane finally gave Henry what he most desired: a male heir, Edward, born in 1537. Tragically, Jane died shortly after giving birth due to postnatal complications. Henry considered Jane to be his only \"true\" wife since she fulfilled his wish for a son.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Reformation and Its Impact</strong><br>\nHenry's reign saw significant changes in English religion through the establishment of the Church of England. The Reformation allowed Henry to consolidate his power and seize wealth from the monasteries. It also led to widespread changes in religious practices and institutions. Under Henry's rule, the Bible was translated into English, making it accessible to the common people for the first time.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
