          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Marriages and Health Decline</strong><br>\nHenry married three more times after Jane's death, to Anne of Cleves, Catherine Howard, and finally to Catherine Parr. None of these marriages produced any children. By this time, Henry's health was in decline. He became obese, with a painful leg ulcer that made it difficult for him to move. His later years were marked by an increasing despotism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Succession</strong><br>\nHenry VIII died on January 28, 1547. His death brought about the end of a notable and tumultuous reign. His son, Edward VI, succeeded him at the age of nine but was sickly and died young. Henry's legacy was complex, marked by his multiple marriages, his role in the Reformation, and his impact on the succession of the English throne.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Henry's Legacy</strong><br>\nHenry VIII's legacy is a mixture of political, religious, and cultural changes. He is remembered for his authoritarian rule, the establishment of a national church, and his impact on the succession of the English monarchy. Henry's decisions, especially in his personal life and marriages, have been the subject of much discussion and controversy over the centuries, leaving an indelible mark on English history.</p>\n"
              },
